*Bytecode* | V-$CHIPVR ($CHIPCN)

Bytecode holds the instructions the compiler produced, together with everything those instructions refer to. It carries no source text, so it is a finished program akin to a binary that a systems language like C may produce, the difference is that the instructions that the Modena compiler produces are specific to the Modena virtual machine and not the underlying processor architecture instruction set or operating system. This allows the bytecode to be processor architecture and operating system independent at least in theory.

You can compile source code to a bytecode file by using ''chippy compile <source>'', or by setting ''Compile'' in a ''combine.chp'' file (see ''chippy doc combine'').

You can see the instructions the compiler produces by disassembling a bytecode file by using ''chippy disasm <file>''.

*The File format*

The Chippy bytecode file format is custom and encodes Chippy / Modena bytecode alongside some identifying and supporting data. It is intentionally kept minimal and 1:1 hash reproducible, this means that the same code, on the same version of Chippy, will give you a binary that is byte identical each time. Nothing about where the source came from is written, so compiling identical code under a different file name gives you the same binary. The file format is handled by the Modena serializer.

'''
+-----------------------------------------------+
|  #!/usr/bin/chippy                            | shebang (optional)
+-----------------------------------------------+
|  F8 43 48 49 50 50 59 FF                      | signature magic, 8 bytes
+-----------------------------------------------+
|  format version                               | u32
+-----------------------------------------------+
|                                               |
|  chunk                                        |
|    code             the instructions          |
|    constants        numbers and strings       |
|    names            globals referred to       |
|    slot count       locals per call frame     |
|    local names      for diagnostics           |
|    functions        name, arity, upvalues     |
|                     and a nested chunk each   |
|                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------+

shebang        -->  part of the format, this is written unless the ''AddShebang = false;'' option is set in combine.
magic          -->  ''0xF8 CHIPPY 0xFF''
format version -->  in case the format the bytecode serializer produces ever needs to change, this indicates the file format version
chunk          -->  the code, and everything the code refers to
constants      -->  only numbers and strings are stored, lists, maps and bytes are built by the instructions themselves when the program runs
'''

*Damaged Files*

A bytecode file is not checked before it runs.

The engine makes a good faith effort to eliminate a large portion of potential file corruption cases with basic checks, but by design, cannot catch them all, since this would be impossible outside of simulating (somehow) the entire program before it runs.

The best way to deal with this once it occurs is to recombine / recompile your binary from source code.

